@startuml gateway-internals
!theme plain
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam nodesep 20
skinparam ranksep 48
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

title The gateway — five parts, and what it deliberately does not know

header
The gateway is the <b>only</b> component holding a client's TCP socket, and it never parses a payload.
Those two facts give ordering and config-driven routing for free.
endheader

actor "client" as client #E3F2FD
component "**services**\ngRPC" as svc #E8F5E9

package "starling-gateway" as GW #FFE9D6 {
  component "**event loop**  «reactor»\nthe one place that awaits socket readiness" as loop #FFFFFF
  component "**listener**\nTCP accept · TLS handshake\nmints the connection id" as listener #FFFFFF
  component "**connection**  [0..*]\none TLS stream · control framing\n«type u16 + len u32»\n—\n**two bounded queues, two policies:**\ncontrol full -> **disconnect**\naudio full -> **drop oldest, count**" as conn #FFFFFF
  component "**router**\nreads the u16 type, looks it up in TOML,\nforwards the payload **verbatim**.\n**No generated stubs. No schemas.**" as router #FFF9C4
  component "**limiter**\na bucket **per route**, never one shared" as lim #FFF9C4
  component "**breaker + shedder**\na circuit breaker per service, and\ninbound shed by `tier` when one is unhealthy" as brk #FFF9C4
  component "**resume buffer**\nsequence number per session,\nreplayed on RESUME" as res #FFE0B2
}

client -down-> listener : TCP 64738
listener *-- conn : on accept
loop --> listener : accept readiness
loop --> conn : read · write readiness
conn --> lim : inbound frame
lim --> brk : if it passed the bucket
brk --> router : if the tier is healthy
router -[#1565C0]-> svc : gRPC, payload unchanged
svc -[#1565C0]-> res : outbound frames
res --> conn : stamped with a sequence number

note right of brk #FFF9C4
  **Deadlines alone fail slowly.** A saturated service makes
  every caller wait the full deadline and *then* fail, burning
  gateway capacity the whole time. Trip the breaker and shed
  at the door instead.
  Shedding uses the same `tier` the readiness logic does:
  essential unhealthy rejects logins, optional unhealthy is
  invisible.
end note

note right of res #FFE0B2
  **Why a replay buffer and not just a reconnect.** Draining a
  pod with 10 000 clients means 10 000 simultaneous full floods
  of every ChannelState and UserState — a self-inflicted DDoS on
  the services behind. A client that resumes from its last
  sequence number replays only the gap.
  The buffer therefore lives **outside** this process, so a
  resuming client can land on any pod. Legacy clients cannot
  resume and take the full flood; zstd on the Fancy stream
  shrinks the same transfer.
end note

note right of router #FFF9C4
  **Why the gateway can stay ignorant.**
  The type is in the *framing*, not the protobuf
  (Server.cpp:2040 writes it as a big-endian u16).
  So routing needs two bytes and a lookup table.
  A new service is a TOML block; this binary
  never changes and links no service's code.
end note

note right of lim #FFF9C4
  **Per route, and never silent.**
  murmur has ONE bucket per user - 1 msg/s,
  burst 5, silent drop. A screen-share start
  emits several signalling messages back to
  back, and this ate the loopback SDP offer in
  most runs: client logged success, server
  logged nothing.
  Fancy clients are told they were throttled.
  Legacy clients keep the silence they expect.
end note

note bottom of conn
  **Single writer per client socket.** Every frame to a client
  leaves through the one process that holds its connection, so
  per-client ordering is preserved by construction - `ChannelState`
  cannot arrive after the `UserState` naming it, whichever service
  produced them. No lane rule and no version counter needed.
end note

note bottom of loop
  **Why the loop is separate from the wire format.**
  "Await readiness and pump gRPC" and "speak Mumble's framing"
  are two jobs. Fused, they become one file nobody can review.
end note

note left of conn #E8F5E9
  **Why the two queue policies differ.** Dropping a control
  message desyncs that client permanently and silently - it
  renders the wrong world forever with nothing in any log.
  Queueing without a bound is a memory DoS. Disconnecting is
  the only outcome both bounded and honest, and reconnect
  already re-syncs from scratch.
  A late **audio** frame is worthless, so drop it and count it.
end note

note as N1 #EEEEEE
  **Not here, on purpose:**
  · the **cipher** - it lives in voice, with the UDP socket,
    so one hop can seal for every listener
  · **audio routing** - clients send UDP straight to voice
  · **any service's schema** - see the router note
end note

legend bottom
| solid, one arrowhead | a direct call inside the gateway |
| <color:#1565C0>blue</color> | gRPC to or from a service |
| filled diamond | creates and owns |
endlegend

@enduml
